Q: WPF strange freezing, related to ICommand.CanExecuteChanged? A: <p>Since you know where delay occurs, why not try something light weight like a <code>DelegateCommand</code>.</p>
<pre><code>    public class DelegateCommand : IDelegateCommand
    {
        public event EventHandler CanExecuteChanged;
        Action _execute;
        Func&lt;bool&gt; _canExecute;

        public DelegateCommand(Action execute, Func&lt;bool&gt; canExecute = null)
        {
            _execute = execute;
            _canExecute = canExecute ?? (() =&gt; true);
        }

        public bool CanExecute(object parameter)
        {
            return _canExecute();
        }

        public void Execute(object parameter)
        {
            _execute();
        }

        public void Refresh()
        {
            CanExecuteChanged.Invoke(this, new EventArgs());
        }
    }
</code></pre>
<p>and the interface is like this:</p>
<pre><code>    public interface IDelegateCommand : ICommand
    {
        void Refresh();
    }
</code></pre>
<p>In your ViewModel you should have something like this:</p>
<pre><code>        public void RefreshCommands(params IDelegateCommand[] commands)
        {
            Dispatcher.BeginInvoke(DispatcherPriority.Normal, new Action(() =&gt;
            {
                foreach (var command in commands)
                    command.Refresh();
            }));
        }
</code></pre>
<p>So now every time you need to update a command, you would call this in property <code>RefreshCommands(SomeCommand);</code>.<br />
This command will be immune to your usual UI changes and will only respond when required.</p>

